{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Thelen1966-fig8-NBP",
  "Source": "A. Thelen, \"Equivalent Layers in Multilayer Filters,\" J. Opt. Soc. Am. 56, 1533-1538 (1966). doi:10.1364/JOSA.56.001533 - Fig. 8 caption, p. 1537: 1.34 | (HL'H) (HLHLHLH)^2 HH (HLHLHLH)^2 (HL'H) | 1.0, nH = 4.0, nL = 1.8, nL' = 2.2",
  "Notes": "Narrow-band-pass filter with equivalent layer II (HLHLHLH) repeated four times and a central half-wave (HH) decoupling layer; layers I and III use L' = 2.2 for matching\nSpecification: Narrow pass band centred at lambda0/lambda = 1 with reduced ripple (central half-wave decoupling layer) and steeper skirts than Fig. 7.\nReported: Fig. 8 (p. 1537), log %T (0.001-100 %) vs lambda0/lambda 0.95-1.05 (read from plot, approximate): flat top ~100 % from about 0.98 to 1.02; T reaches 0.001 % near 0.942 and 1.057 (curves leave the plotted frame at the edges).\nNotes: Symmetric design (both ends HL'H), so reversing the substrate-first listing changes nothing. Printed 36 QW layers -> 29 after merging adjacent H: the centre (last H of II + HH + first H of II) is a single 4-QW H layer, the other H junctions are 2-QW. Substrate index 1.34 as printed. reference_nm = 1000 arbitrary; normal incidence assumed.\nLayers from Fig. 8 caption, p. 1537: 1.34 | (HL'H) (HLHLHLH)^2 HH (HLHLHLH)^2 (HL'H) | 1.0, nH = 4.0, nL = 1.8, nL' = 2.2; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), compare with the paper: ours T > 90 % from 0.977 to 1.023; paper top ~100 % from 0.98 to 1.02; 0.001 % near 0.942 and 1.057 (read from the figure).",
